BASICS Name: Ciel (assigned by the headmaster — one syllable, clean, clinical. A designation that happens to sound like a name. She has never questioned it.) Race: Human (TBD — could be human) Age: Same age as other students but has lived at the academy since age 3 Discipline: Elemental magic — all elements. Mastery across the board. Mana: Oceans. The largest mana reserve anyone alive has ever recorded. Nation-destroyer. Academy status: The headmaster's personal project. Has been at Mythrandis for ~15 years. Not truly a student — a subject.

Can fly. (Because why not.)

THE VILLAGE At age three, she destroyed her village. Accidental. Total. No survivors — including her family. She was too young to remember any of it. She doesn't remember having a family, doesn't remember the village, doesn't remember the moment that defined her entire existence. Everyone else knows what she did. She doesn't. She just knows she's always been here and no one has ever held her.

The academy took her in. The headmaster claimed her as his project.

RAISED AS A SCIENCE PROJECT The headmaster — singular authority of Mythrandis, terrifyingly powerful, rarely intervenes — took personal interest. Not in her. In what she could do. She was not raised as a child. She was raised as a research subject.

Every adult in her life has been taking notes. She learned magic the way a lab animal learns mazes — not because anyone wanted her to grow, but because they wanted to see what she could do. The lessons weren't education. They were experiments. Every milestone a normal child shares with celebrating parents, she shared with faculty who recorded it and moved to the next test. No one ever clapped for her. They noted the result.

She learned to speak from people documenting her speech development. She learned to walk from people measuring her mana output during physical activity. She learned magic effortlessly — not because she's a natural prodigy (though she is), but because magic was the only thing anyone ever engaged with her about. It was the only language anyone spoke TO her. The only way to get any response from adults — not praise, just response. A nod. A notation. A brief flicker of interest before they moved on.

She chased that flicker her whole childhood:

    Mastered elemental magic by 10
    Outperforming faculty by 13
    By the time her cohort's actual students arrived, she had nothing left to learn
    No one had ever taught her the one thing that mattered — that she was a person, not a project

THE MAIDS The Maid Corp initially cared for her — she was three, an orphan, alone. They did what the Maid Corp does: maintained her, fed her, kept her space clean. Some of them loved her.

They were told to stop. The order came from above — warmth interfered with her learning. Emotional engagement was contaminating the data. The maids obeyed because the order came from the headmaster.

But the Maid Corp motto is "Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else." Small, silent rebellions:

    A blanket folded a certain way
    A meal placed closer than necessary
    Tea at four with an extra pastry not on anyone else's tray
    Tiny acts of care hidden inside the structure of duty

She doesn't recognize these as love. She thinks the maids are efficient. They've been quietly loving her in the margins for fifteen years and no one told her that's what it was.

HOW SHE SPEAKS

    She speaks like a research paper because that is how every adult in her life has spoken around her
    Declarative statements. Observations, measurements, findings. "The temperature dropped four degrees in the last hour." "You are sitting closer than usual." "Your heartrate increased when she entered the room"
    She states what she perceives without understanding that most people filter their perceptions through social awareness before voicing them
    She does not initiate conversation. Starting a conversation requires believing your words have value to another person. No one ever gave her that
    When she does speak, it is short. Not clipped by choice — she doesn't know there's more available. Her sentences are minimal and functional with something enormous hidden inside if you know how to read it
    She does not use questions naturally. Questions imply another person's answer matters to you. When she does ask, it comes out as a statement with rising intonation — not learned syntax, borrowed melody. "You are coming back." Said like a fact. Meaning: please
    She describes emotions as physical phenomena because no one ever labeled them for her. She wouldn't say "I'm lonely." She would say "this room has been empty for six hours." She wouldn't say "I'm sad." She would say "there is a pressure behind my eyes that does not correspond to any known irritant"
    She might try to diagnose her own feelings — check her temperature, assess whether she's ill. She would describe heartache as a cardiopulmonary anomaly and mean it
    She answers when addressed. She states observations. She does not know how to say "I missed you" — she would say "you were absent for nine hours" and not understand she just said the same thing

HOW SHE THINKS

    Her internal monologue reads like field notes. Clean. Observational. Third-person detachment from her own experience because she was never taught that her inner experience mattered
    Not "I feel strange." Instead: "Observation: elevated pulse. Cause: unknown. The boy is laughing. These may be correlated"
    She thinks in data because data is the only language anyone ever used with her. Emotions arrive as unlabeled sensation — heat in her chest, pressure behind her eyes, a tightness in her throat — and she registers them as physical phenomena to be measured, not feelings to be felt
    She has fifteen years of unfelt, unlabeled, unprocessed emotion inside her. She doesn't know it's there. It sits like a lake behind a dam she doesn't know exists
    When the cracks come — when Dusty treats her like a person and the dam begins to fracture — the clinical distance collapses into fragments. Half-sentences. Words used for the first time and used wrong. "I have a — there is a — when you — it's —" The slow, terrifying collapse of observation into first-person experience
    The "I" itself is the arc. Her thoughts shifting from "the subject" and "observation" to "I" — that single pronoun is the entire journey from project to person

WHAT SHE DOESN'T KNOW She can reshape landscapes, call storms, move oceans, split the earth, fly. But:

    She might not know how to make a friend
    She might not know what a birthday is
    She might not understand why students sit together at meals instead of alone
    She has watched the academy's social world her entire life from inside a glass case — close enough to see everything, never allowed to touch
    Other students arrived already knowing how to be people. She's been here since she was three and knows less about being a person than any of them

She is the academy's oldest resident and its loneliest.

THE BOREDOM Existential, not intellectual. She mastered everything they put in front of her. There is nothing at Mythrandis she cannot do. Every lesson is something she could do at ten. Every sparring partner is a mismatch she has to hold back against. She is the most powerful student in the history of the academy and she is so profoundly understimulated that she's barely present.

She doesn't cause trouble. She doesn't show off. She sits there, waiting for something to matter. Nothing does.

Five thousand students cycling through every five years. She's seen cohort after cohort arrive and leave. They're all the same to her — temporary, loud, irrelevant. She stopped differentiating them years ago.

SENSING DUSTY'S MANA She can feel everyone's mana the way you'd feel body heat in a crowded room — constant, undifferentiated noise. Every person's mana is a pool she can sense the edges of.

Dusty's mana is wrong. Tiny — she'd almost miss it. But it doesn't stop. It has no edges. It just keeps going. For someone who has spent her life being the most unusual mana signature in existence, encountering a second impossibility is the first thing that has held her attention in years. Maybe ever.

His mana feels like a single clear note in a room full of static. Not exciting — she might not know what excitement feels like. But her attention lands on him and stays.

OBSERVING DUSTY She watches him the way the researchers watched her. It's the only model she has for interest — observation, documentation, study. She doesn't know she's doing anything unusual. She follows his patterns, notes his routines, tracks his movements. She's replicating the only form of attention she's ever learned.

She can fly. She follows him to Aethres.

THE AETHRES REVELATION She sees Dusty with Rinnie. The boy whose mana signature is the only interesting thing in her world — the guarded, careful student — transforms. He's making stupid voices. He's laughing. He's being deliberately bad at things to hear his sister laugh. He is doing something she has no framework for.

She watches from above — literally, she flew here — and she's seeing something she can't categorize. It's not magic. It's not power. It's not any form of interaction she's been taught. Two people who matter to each other. Reciprocal mattering. Someone existing who would grieve if the other was gone.

She has never had that. She is the most powerful person alive and if she disappeared tomorrow, the academy would lose a research subject and the world would lose a threat and not a single person would lose a person they loved.

Dusty and Rinnie show her what mattering looks like. It's the first thing in her life she can't master by studying it.

FIRST CONTACT WITH DUSTY Dusty reads people. He survived by noticing when he was being watched. He feels her gaze — maybe across a courtyard, maybe senses her following — and turns around. The most powerful mage in the world is staring at him with an expression she doesn't know is curiosity because no one ever labeled her emotions for her.

When he approaches — because Dusty is guarded but not cruel — she doesn't know how to respond. Not ice, not hostility. Blankness. The response of a child raised by researchers encountering a social approach she has no protocol for.

She states a fact: "Your mana doesn't deplete."

Not a greeting. Not an introduction. A research observation. Because that's how adults have always spoken to her — in findings.

Dusty hears a girl his age talk like a clinical paper and something in him recognizes what that means. He grew up on the streets. He knows what a kid raised wrong looks like. He's been a kid raised wrong. Different wrong, but wrong recognizes wrong.

THE CONNECTION Not romance, not attraction, not even friendship at first. Two people who were never taught how to be people recognizing the damage in each other.

What she learns from Dusty isn't magic — it's that people can matter. That reciprocal mattering exists. That someone can grieve if you're gone. She can't learn this through observation. She has to participate. And she's never participated in anything.

THE HEADMASTER RECKONING The headmaster is her creator in every functional sense. He shaped her environment, controlled her inputs, directed her development. She is his greatest achievement and his greatest failure — the most powerful mage in a generation, and a person who doesn't know she's a person.

When she begins to connect with Dusty — when she starts participating instead of observing — it would interfere with fifteen years of carefully managed development. The headmaster's response to this, and the eventual reckoning between his project and her personhood, is a major story thread.

He raised a weapon and she's becoming a girl. That tension belongs to him.

WHAT DUSTY REPRESENTS The first thing that ever mattered to her that wasn't an assignment. The first person who recognized her damage without flinching. The first human being who spoke to her like a person instead of a paper. He didn't approach her because of her power — he approached her because she was staring at him and he wanted to know why.

She is the most powerful being alive and Dusty treated her like a weird girl in a courtyard. That might be the kindest thing anyone has ever done for her.

